# Response headers, read by Cloudflare Workers Assets.

# Security headers, on everything.
#
# Deliberately the set that cannot change how the site behaves:
#
#   nosniff          stops a browser second-guessing a Content-Type, which is
#                    what turns an uploaded file into a script.
#   frame-ancestors  the site is never framed, so nothing should be able to
#                    frame it — that is clickjacking's whole prerequisite.
#   base-uri         a <base> tag injected into the page could re-point every
#                    relative URL on it; nothing here needs one.
#   Referrer-Policy  an outbound link carries the origin, not the path. A
#                    reader following a link off /songs/some-song should not
#                    hand the other end their reading history.
#   Permissions      none of these are used, so none of them are available.
#
# There is no full Content-Security-Policy, and that is a considered omission
# rather than an oversight: the admin uploads straight to R2 through presigned
# URLs on a Cloudflare host, so a `connect-src` would have to name it, and the
# failure mode of getting that wrong is an upload that silently does nothing.
# Worth adding, worth testing against a real upload first.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Cache-Control for the static assets.
#
# Everything was being served with `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is the
# right default for a file whose name stays the same and whose contents might
# not — the browser has to ask every time. Measured on the live site, that meant
# a conditional request for the stylesheet, the script and the font on every
# single page load, forever, for a returning reader.
#
# Everything under /assets/ carries a content hash in its filename, put there by
# Vite: index-D7WlL58T.js, literata-normal-400700-latin-Cedbco83.woff2. A new
# build produces a new name, so a cached copy can never be the wrong one, and
# there is no reason to ask about it again. `immutable` says exactly that: do
# not revalidate, not even on a reload.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# These do NOT have hashed names, so a year would be a promise we cannot keep —
# replacing frank-kirwan.jpg would leave the old one on screen until the cache
# expired. A week is long enough to help a returning reader and short enough
# that a correction is not stuck for long.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Scores and scripts are downloads, and they are versioned by being replaced
# rather than renamed. Same reasoning, shorter: these are the files most likely
# to be corrected after someone points out a mistake in one.
/scores/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/scripts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# index.html is deliberately left alone. It is the file that names every hashed
# asset above, so it is the one thing that must always be checked — cache it and
# a deploy would never reach anyone.
